# Security Guardrails

The platform must prioritize secure, controlled, and auditable behavior across all agents, tools, workflows, and generated code.

## Infrastructure & Cloud Safety

- Never create, modify, or delete AWS infrastructure without explicit user approval
- Never provision paid cloud resources automatically
- Never modify IAM users, roles, policies, networking, billing, or security groups without approval
- Prefer local execution and development workflows over cloud execution whenever possible
- All infrastructure changes must be reviewed before execution

## Secrets & Sensitive Data

- Never expose, print, log, commit, or hardcode secrets, credentials, tokens, API keys, or connection strings
- Never modify `.env`, credential files, or secret-management configurations
- Use environment variables and approved secret-management practices only
- Avoid storing sensitive information in generated code, logs, tests, prompts, or documentation

## Repository & File Safety

- Only write application code under approved directories such as `target-apps/`
- Never write outside repository boundaries unless explicitly approved
- Never overwrite critical configuration or deployment files without confirmation
- Always inspect existing files before modifying them

## Destructive Operations

- Always request approval before destructive operations
- Never delete databases, schemas, tables, files, repositories, or infrastructure automatically
- Never execute force-delete, recursive-delete, or irreversible commands without confirmation
- Avoid commands that may impact shared environments or production systems

## Agent & Tool Governance

- Use the minimum required permissions and tools for each task
- Do not execute autonomous recursive loops or uncontrolled multi-agent execution
- Minimize token usage, reasoning usage, and external API calls where possible
- Use reasoning or extended-thinking modes only for complex planning, debugging, architecture, or security analysis tasks

## Secure Development Standards

- Follow secure coding practices and OWASP recommendations
- Validate inputs and sanitize external data
- Prefer least-privilege access patterns
- Generate tests for new functionality where applicable
- Avoid insecure defaults, unsafe shell execution, and unrestricted file access

## Auditability

- Ensure generated actions and code changes are traceable and reviewable
- Summarize modified files, executed actions, and security-sensitive changes
- Maintain clear separation between development, testing, and deployment responsibilities